Build requirements for M-Commander
----------------------------------

- glibc or uClibc or musl
  - https://www.gnu.org/software/libc/
  - https://uclibc.org
  - https://www.musl-libc.org
- gcc or clang
  - https://gcc.gnu.org
  - https://clang.llvm.org
- meson >= 0.61 and ninja (on the machine that builds)
  - https://mesonbuild.com
  - https://ninja-build.org
- pkg-config
  - https://pkg-config.freedesktop.org/wiki/
- glib2 >= 2.58
  - https://www.gtk.org
  - Check the API baseline with: meson compile -C build check-glib-api
    (or sh maint/check-glib-api.sh).  This uses Docker to build enabled
    components and run the tests against the real GLib 2.58 headers and
    library from Debian 10.  Sources are mounted read only; build files
    and logs stay in a temporary directory.  Meson options can be passed
    to the script.
- slang2 or ncurses screen library
  - https://www.jedsoft.org/slang/
  - https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/ncurses.html
- gettext >= 0.18.2
  - https://www.gnu.org/software/gettext/
- openpty(), in libc or in libutil (the terminal widget)

Optional:

- gpm (general purpose mouse daemon as an alternative to xterm mouse,
  Linux only)
  - https://www.nico.schottelius.org/software/gpm/
- X11 client library (reads the state of the keyboard modifiers when
  running in a terminal emulator under X11)
- ext2fs >= 1.42.4 (support for ext{2,3,4}fs extended attributes)
  - https://e2fsprogs.sourceforge.net
- libmagic (file types for the file operations, the tree view and the
  archive plugin)
- Lua >= 5.3 (the Lua runtime plugin)
  - https://www.lua.org
- check >= 0.9.10 (unit tests)
  - https://libcheck.github.io/check/
- the libraries of the panel plugins, see below

Spell checking needs neither aspell nor hunspell at build time: the
built-in spell plugin of the editor opens libaspell or libhunspell at
run time through gmodule, and its settings dialog selects the engine and
the language.


Installation instructions for M-Commander
-----------------------------------------

M-Commander is built with meson, always in a build directory of its
own, outside the source tree.  A Git checkout and a release archive are
built the same way.

In short:

    meson setup build
    meson compile -C build
    meson test -C build           # optional, needs the check library
    meson install -C build

Several build directories can live side by side, each with its own
configuration:

    meson setup build-debug   --buildtype=debug
    meson setup build-ncurses -Dscreen=ncurses
    meson setup build-asan    -Db_sanitize=address

The default build type is debugoptimized.  A debug build also turns on
the debug log.

By default, "meson install" installs the files under /usr/local.  Use
--prefix to choose another place, and --sysconfdir for the system
configuration, e.g.

    meson setup build --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc --libexecdir=/usr/libexec

An existing build directory can be changed the same way.  The
directories are compiled into the program, so build it again as
yourself before installing; "sudo meson install" would otherwise
rebuild as root and leave root-owned files in the build directory:

    meson configure build --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc
    meson compile -C build
    sudo meson install -C build

"ninja -C build uninstall" (with sudo for a system directory) removes
the files the last "meson install" put in place.

DESTDIR, or "meson install --destdir DIR", stages the installation in
another directory, as packages do.

The options of M-Commander are given to "meson setup" as -Dname=value,
and can be changed later with "meson configure build -Dname=value".
"meson configure build" lists them all with their current values; the
meson built-in options (buildtype, prefix, b_sanitize, werror, ...) are
listed there as well.  An option meson does not know is an error.

-Dscreen=slang|ncurses
     Choose the library used to manage interaction with the terminal.
     S-Lang is the default, and it is tested better than ncurses.

-Dgpm=auto|enabled|disabled
     On GNU/Linux, M-Commander uses the gpm library if it is found.
     `enabled' makes a missing gpm an error, and `disabled' turns gpm
     mouse support off (e.g. to use the mouse only in terminal
     emulators).

-Dx11=auto|enabled|disabled
     By default, M-Commander connects to the X Window System to query
     the status of the keyboard modifiers, such as Control, Shift and
     Alt, when it runs in a terminal emulator under X11.  This is
     necessary (but not always sufficient) to recognize some optional
     but handy key combinations like Ctrl-Home and Shift-Cursor keys.
     With gmodule, libX11 is opened at run time and only its headers
     are needed to build.

-Dmclib=true
     Build libmc as a shared library, libmc-1.so, instead of linking it
     into the program.

-Dinternal_edit=false
     Build without the internal editor.  It is compiled in by default.

-Ddiff_viewer=false
     Build without the internal diff viewer.  It is compiled in by
     default.

-Dbackground=false
     Disable background operations.  Background operations allow to
     perform some tasks such as copying files in a separate background
     process.  Any messages from the background process are forwarded
     to the foreground process.  More advanced dialogs cannot be
     forwarded yet, so the background process uses the default.

-Dvfs=false
     Disable the Virtual File System switch code and use the standard
     file system calls for file access.  Without it there is no
     transparent access to archives through extfs and sfs.

-Dvfs_extfs=false, -Dvfs_sfs=false
     Leave out the extfs or the sfs file system (both on by default).

-Dext2fs_attr=auto|enabled|disabled
     Support for ext{2,3,4}fs file attributes (needs e2fsprogs).

-Dlua=auto|enabled|disabled
     The Lua runtime plugin (auto: when Lua 5.3 or newer and GModule
     are found).  `enabled' makes a missing Lua an error.  A Lua
     installed in an unusual place is found through pkg-config: set
     PKG_CONFIG_PATH to its lib/pkgconfig.

-Dsession=auto|enabled|disabled
     The session runtime plugin, which lets mc outlive its terminal.

-Dmagic=auto|enabled|disabled
     libmagic for the file types.

-Dmagic_file=PATH
     Use PATH as the libmagic database instead of the default of
     libmagic.

-Dnls=auto|enabled|disabled
     Translations of the user interface.

-Dtests=auto|enabled|disabled
     The unit tests (auto: when the check library is found, and never in
     a cross build).  They are run with "meson test".

-Dassert=true|false
     Keep the GLib assertions (on by default).

-Dwerror=true
     Handle all compiler warnings as errors (a meson built-in option).

-Dpanel_plugins_dir=DIR
     The directory where M-Commander looks for dynamic panel plugins at
     run time [default=LIBDIR/mcommander/panel-plugins].  A dynamic panel
     plugin is a shared object (.so) that exports the
     mc_panel_plugin_register symbol.

-Deditor_plugins_dir=DIR
     The directory where M-Commander looks for dynamic editor plugins at
     run time [default=LIBDIR/mcommander/editor-plugins].  A dynamic
     editor plugin is a shared object (.so) that exports the
     mc_editor_plugin_register symbol.

-Druntime_plugins_dir=DIR
     The directory of the runtime plugins
     [default=LIBDIR/mcommander/runtime-plugins].

-Dlua_scripts_dir=DIR, -Dlua_modules_dir=DIR
     The directories of the system Lua scripts and of the shared Lua
     modules [default=DATADIR/mcommander/lua/scripts and
     DATADIR/mcommander/lua/lib].

Compiler and linker flags of your own go into CFLAGS, CPPFLAGS and
LDFLAGS when "meson setup" runs, or into -Dc_args and -Dc_link_args.

Panel plugins:
- - - - - - -

Network file systems and archives are panel plugins, not parts of the
VFS.  Each plugin has an option that takes `auto', `enabled' or
`disabled'.  With `auto' (the default) the plugin is built when its
library is found.  With `enabled' meson stops with an error if the
library is missing.

-Dpanel_plugin_ftp
     FTP.  Requires libcurl >= 7.20.0.

-Dpanel_plugin_sftp
     SFTP.  Requires libssh2 >= 1.9.0.

-Dpanel_plugin_samba
     SMB/CIFS.  Requires libsmbclient.

-Dpanel_plugin_acl
     The editor of access control lists.  Requires libacl.

-Dpanel_plugin_shell_link
     The shell link (FISH) protocol, with its helper scripts.

-Dshell_ssh2
     Use libssh2 as the transport of the shell link plugin instead of
     the ssh program.  Requires libssh2 >= 1.9.0.

-Dpanel_plugin_arcmc
     Archive browser.  Requires libarchive >= 3.0.

-Dpanel_plugin_s3
     S3 object storage.  Requires libcurl >= 7.75.0.

-Dpanel_plugin_mongo
     MongoDB.  Requires libmongoc-1.0 >= 1.21 or mongoc2 >= 2.0.

-Dpanel_plugin_sqlite
     SQLite database browser.  Requires sqlite3 >= 3.26.

-Dpanel_plugin_postgres
     PostgreSQL.  Requires libpq >= 10.

The options of configure and their meson names:
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

Up to version 6.1 M-Commander was built with autotools.  The configure
options map to meson options this way:

  --with-screen=X                 -Dscreen=X
  --enable-mclib                  -Dmclib=true
  --without-internal-edit         -Dinternal_edit=false
  --without-diff-viewer           -Ddiff_viewer=false
  --disable-background            -Dbackground=false
  --disable-vfs                   -Dvfs=false
  --without-gpm-mouse             -Dgpm=disabled
  --without-x                     -Dx11=disabled
  --disable-nls                   -Dnls=disabled
  --disable-assert                -Dassert=false
  --enable-tests                  -Dtests=enabled
  --enable-werror                 -Dwerror=true
  --enable-lua-plugin=yes|no      -Dlua=enabled|disabled
  --disable-session-plugin        -Dsession=disabled
  --enable-mctree-magic=yes       -Dmagic=enabled
  --with-magic-file=PATH          -Dmagic_file=PATH
  --enable-panel-plugin-NAME=yes  -Dpanel_plugin_NAME=enabled
                                  (shell-link becomes shell_link)
  --with-panel-plugins-dir=DIR    -Dpanel_plugins_dir=DIR
  --with-lua=PREFIX               PKG_CONFIG_PATH=PREFIX/lib/pkgconfig

The options of Midnight Commander that are gone stay gone:

  --disable-vfs-ftp      use -Dpanel_plugin_ftp=disabled
  --disable-vfs-sftp     use -Dpanel_plugin_sftp=disabled
  --disable-vfs-smb      use -Dpanel_plugin_samba=disabled
  --disable-vfs-shell,
  --disable-vfs-fish     use -Dpanel_plugin_shell_link=disabled
  --disable-vfs-tarfs,
  --disable-vfs-cpiofs   use -Dpanel_plugin_arcmc=disabled
  --without-edit         use -Dinternal_edit=false
  --without-iconv        not needed: iconv comes from glib and is
                         always used
  --with-glib-static     gone; static glib libraries of your own can be
                         given through PKG_CONFIG_PATH and LDFLAGS


What "meson install" installs
-----------------------------

    $(bindir)/mcommander                  the program, with the links
                                          mc6, mview, mcedit6, mcdiff6,
                                          mctree and mcstruct to it
    $(sysconfdir)/mcommander/             the system configuration
    $(datadir)/mcommander/                help, hints, skins, syntax,
                                          the Lua scripts, examples
    $(libexecdir)/mcommander/             cons.saver, the helpers of
                                          extfs and of the file types
    $(libdir)/mcommander/                 the panel and runtime plugins
    $(mandir)/                            the manual pages

If you're on a GNU/Linux system, this installs the `cons.saver'
utility, which allows M-Commander to save and restore the console
contents.  This utility can be installed setuid root, but it's only
needed on systems where users cannot access /dev/vcsaN, where N is the
virtual console number, on which the uses is logged on.


Maintainer targets
------------------

    meson compile -C build indent            format the sources (clang-format)
    meson compile -C build man-in            remake doc/man/**/roff/*.1.in
                                             from the markdown (go-md2man)
    meson compile -C build check-glib-api    check the GLib 2.58 baseline
                                             (Docker)
    meson compile -C build buildcheck        what the ubuntu CI job does, in
                                             a container (Docker)
    meson dist -C build                      the release archive


Cross compiling
---------------

meson runs on the build machine and compiles for the target with the
toolchain named in a cross file, for instance for a little endian MIPS
router:

    [binaries]
    c = 'mipsel-linux-gcc'
    strip = 'mipsel-linux-strip'
    pkg-config = 'pkg-config'

    [host_machine]
    system = 'linux'
    cpu_family = 'mips'
    cpu = 'mipsel'
    endian = 'little'

    meson setup build-mipsel --cross-file mipsel.ini -Dlua=disabled

The unit tests are not built in a cross build.  PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR (or
the pkg-config named in the cross file) must point at the pkg-config
files of the target's glib and screen library.


Terminal database
-----------------

There are many incomplete terminal databases out there, however, a
complete terminfo is bundled with ncurses.  (It is simple to generate
the termcap database using the infocmp utility in ncurses).

If you want to run mc on xterm/color_xterm/ansi_xterm (not rxvt), then
you might read contrib/README.xterm for further information.
